    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Okay, I see. So it's still pretty wide open. Are there any specific things I should look out for in these kinds of clauses that might be \"sneaky\" or meant to trap someone into paying way more than they expected? Like, are there certain words that act as red flags?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When it comes to indemnification clauses, there are some common pitfalls to watch out for. One thing to be cautious of is the use of vague language, such as \"all losses, damages, liabilities,\" as we discussed earlier. This can make it difficult to determine what exactly you're liable for.\n\nAnother red flag is the inclusion of \"reasonable attorneys' fees\" in the clause. While this might seem like a reasonable provision, it can actually be a trap. If the clause is written broadly enough, you could be liable for not only your own attorneys' fees but also the Disclosing Party's fees, even if you're not at fault.\n\nAdditionally, some clauses might include \"consequential damages\" or \"indirect damages,\" which can be more difficult to quantify and may not be directly related to your breach of the agreement. These types of damages can be particularly problematic, as they can lead to much higher liability than you might expect.\n\nIt's also worth watching out for clauses that use \"willful misconduct\" or \"gross negligence\" as a basis for liability. These terms can be subjective and may be open to interpretation, which can lead to disputes and increased liability.\n\nFinally, be wary of clauses that include \"any and all\" language",
